

TTYS(5)              UNIX Programmer's Manual              TTYS(5)



NAME
     ttys - terminal line database

DESCRIPTION
     The file /etc/ttys lists the terminal lines init(8) manages.
     Each line describes one tty:

          name   getty   type   status

     Fields are separated by spaces or tabs.  Blank lines and text
     from a `#' to the end of line are ignored.  For example:

          # name  getty              type    status
          console /bin/sh            vt100   on secure
          ttyv1   /bin/getty         vt100   off secure

     name    the tty's device name, e.g. "ttyp0".  The physical
             console is named "console", which is what ttyname(3)
             reports for it.  init(8) opens /dev/console for that
             line like any other, in the child that will run on
             it: the open is what binds the controlling terminal.

     getty   the command init(8) runs on the line.  This is
             usually getty(8), which prompts and execs login(1);
             pointing a line directly at a shell skips the
             login prompt.

     type    the terminal type, used to set TERM in the
             environment login(1) builds.

     status  a list of words: "on" enables the line (init runs
             getty on it) and "secure" allows root to log in on
             it directly.  A line without "on" is not started.

VIRTUAL CONSOLES
     ttyv1 and ttyv2 are virtual consoles: independent screens on
     the one physical display, each running its own getty(8) and
     login(1).  Switch between them with OPTION-N -- OPTION-1 for
     the console, OPTION-2 for ttyv1, OPTION-3 for ttyv2.  Ctrl-N
     is not used: on a real IIgs keyboard it never reaches the
     handler that would see it.

     Turning a line "on" is not free.  Each costs one process slot
     for its getty and another for the session shell, so the
     shipped three-console configuration needs kernel.nproc to be
     at least 11 (cfg/system.cfg).  tools/check_stack_budget.py
     ties the two together and fails the build by name if a line
     is enabled that the process table cannot fund, rather than
     letting the machine discover it at login time.

     The session shells share one bank-0 stack region between
     them, so a third console does not cost a third shell's stack.
     See docs/b3-stack-swap-scope.md.

     A line that is "off" is still worth listing.  login(1) reads
     this file for TERM and for the secure flag whoever opened the
     line, so an unlisted line silently gets login's vt100
     fallback and no root check at all.  The pty slaves are listed
     for exactly that reason:

          ttyp0   none               fgxpty  off secure
          ttyp1   none               fgxpty  off

     init never starts a getty on a pty -- the session belongs to
     whoever opened the master -- so their getty field is "none"
     and their status is "off".  ttyp1 is deliberately not secure,
     so login(1)'s root gate has a line it can be tested on.

     Two narrowings from BSD's /etc/ttys, both because FGX has no
     window system: the getty field is a single command path, not
     a quoted string with arguments (BSD allows "getty std.9600");
     and there is no window field.

     Like BSD's, this file carries no commentary beyond its header
     line, and the reason here is not only convention.  login(1)
     reads /etc/ttys from the top on every login, before it can
     print the password prompt, and it reads a line at a time --
     so anything written in the file is paid for by every user on
     every login.  A 4 KB commented version cost 2.6 seconds per
     login (apple/fgx#50).  Explanations belong in this page.

FILES
     /etc/ttys

SEE ALSO
     getty(8), init(8), login(1)



